WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:06.480
Okay, so now let's check quickly the syntax of a view.

00:06.480 --> 00:09.360
So now we have a query like select from where.

00:09.360 --> 00:12.200
So this is a query a simple Select statement.

00:12.200 --> 00:18.560
But now in order to create a view an object in database we have to go and use a DDL command create.

00:18.560 --> 00:22.840
So we're going to say create view because we want to create a view.

00:23.080 --> 00:24.760
Then the name of the view.

00:24.760 --> 00:28.920
And then it's like the CTE we say as and then double parentheses.

00:28.920 --> 00:30.320
So as you can see it's very simple.

00:30.320 --> 00:35.440
And we call this a DDL command where we are telling the database go and create a view.

00:35.440 --> 00:38.480
And the logic of the view comes from this query.

00:38.480 --> 00:39.440
So it's very simple.

00:39.440 --> 00:42.080
This is how you can create views in database okay.

00:42.120 --> 00:43.440
So now let's have the following task.

00:43.480 --> 00:49.000
And it says find the running total of sales for each month I'm going to start this task by solving it

00:49.000 --> 00:50.000
using the CTE.

00:50.240 --> 00:54.000
So first I'm going to go and do few aggregations on the top of the month.

00:54.000 --> 00:55.560
So let's go and select.

00:55.560 --> 00:56.360
So now what do we need.

00:56.360 --> 00:58.040
We need the order date.

00:58.040 --> 01:04.560
But we need it as a month I'm going to go and use the date They drank it like this and say, okay,

01:04.600 --> 01:12.120
I would like to have the date as the granularity of month, so let's go and call it Order month.

01:12.160 --> 01:14.280
And now after that we're going to do a few aggregations.

01:14.280 --> 01:18.120
Like for example, let's go and get the sum of sales.

01:18.440 --> 01:20.560
And we're going to call it total sales.

01:20.560 --> 01:21.800
And that's it for the start.

01:21.800 --> 01:27.720
So now let's go and call it from the table sales orders and group by.

01:28.200 --> 01:30.440
And we are grouping by by the month.

01:30.600 --> 01:31.880
So something like this.

01:31.880 --> 01:33.080
Let's go and execute it.

01:33.080 --> 01:35.800
And now for this we get for each month the total sales.

01:35.840 --> 01:40.040
And now the next step that we have to go and calculate the running total for the sales.

01:40.040 --> 01:41.880
This is of course not the running total.

01:41.880 --> 01:44.480
So that means either we can go and use subqueries.

01:44.480 --> 01:47.400
So this means this is our first step and we need a second step.

01:47.400 --> 01:51.040
So either use queries or Ctes I will go with the CTE over here.

01:51.040 --> 01:57.240
So I'm going to say with city and monthly summary.

01:58.040 --> 01:59.880
And we're going to define it like this.

02:00.360 --> 02:03.560
And now what we're going to do we're going to go and define the main query.

02:03.560 --> 02:05.080
So the main query can be simple.

02:05.080 --> 02:08.280
So select and let's go and get the order month.

02:08.560 --> 02:11.120
And now we have to build the running total.

02:11.120 --> 02:13.480
So we're going to go and use the window function.

02:13.480 --> 02:16.640
So sum total sales.

02:16.800 --> 02:20.080
And then we're going to say over we don't have to partition the data.

02:20.080 --> 02:26.680
We will just sort it by the order month and we can leave it ascending.

02:26.680 --> 02:29.240
So this is the running total.

02:30.840 --> 02:34.480
And we have to go and select of course our CTE from here.

02:34.800 --> 02:36.680
So let's go and execute it.

02:36.680 --> 02:38.400
And with that we are getting the running total.

02:38.440 --> 02:43.080
Of course we can go and add the total sales in the output in order to understand the results.

02:43.080 --> 02:46.680
So here in the output we are just building a cumulative sales.

02:46.680 --> 02:48.480
So for this scope everything is fine.

02:48.480 --> 02:49.600
We are using the CTE.

02:50.000 --> 02:54.600
But now imagine that this logic is important for multiple queries.

02:54.600 --> 02:59.480
So it's really nice to have such a report where we are aggregating the data at the level of the month.

02:59.480 --> 03:02.880
And this could be used from different users and different queries.

03:02.880 --> 03:08.080
So now we say how about to put this logic in one view so that everyone can access it?

03:08.080 --> 03:11.400
And we don't have to repeat the same aggregations over and over?

03:11.440 --> 03:16.640
And now before we put it in view, someone comes and say, how about to add one more aggregation so

03:16.640 --> 03:18.720
that not only the total sales we can order?

03:18.760 --> 03:23.120
So now before we put it as view, maybe some other user says, you know what, we would like to have

03:23.120 --> 03:24.000
one more aggregation.

03:24.000 --> 03:25.200
Not only the total sales.

03:25.200 --> 03:28.360
Let's make the scope a little bit bigger so that everyone can believe it.

03:28.360 --> 03:33.120
So for example, we can go over here and say, you know what, let's go and add the total number of

03:33.120 --> 03:33.720
orders.

03:33.960 --> 03:40.920
So we can go over here and say count and let's get the order ID and say, this is the total orders or

03:40.920 --> 03:42.280
maybe some other sales.

03:42.280 --> 03:48.960
Let's get the quantities as well so we can go and summarize the quantity like this.

03:49.000 --> 03:51.560
And we call it total quantities.

03:52.320 --> 03:56.000
So with that we are like doing a lot of aggregations on the month level.

03:56.000 --> 03:57.880
Let's go and execute only the CTE.

03:57.920 --> 04:04.400
So now we have really nice report that is based on the months and can be used from many different queries.

04:04.400 --> 04:07.920
So now what we're going to do we're going to take this and put it in a view.

04:07.960 --> 04:11.840
Let's go and select only this logic and create a new query.

04:11.880 --> 04:14.120
And now what we're going to do we're going to put our query here.

04:14.120 --> 04:17.840
And we have to create now the DDL in order to create a view.

04:18.040 --> 04:20.400
So it's going to be like this create view.

04:20.440 --> 04:23.960
Let's give it the name maybe starts with the V underscore.

04:24.080 --> 04:26.880
And this is going to be the monthly summary.

04:27.240 --> 04:28.960
So this is the name of the view.

04:29.240 --> 04:32.680
And as then we put everything in parentheses.

04:33.000 --> 04:34.880
It's like you are building a CTE.

04:35.080 --> 04:41.040
So we have here our logic and here is our DDL query in order to create the view.

04:41.080 --> 04:43.000
So now let's go and execute it.

04:43.040 --> 04:46.640
Now as you can see in the output it says only that the command is completed.

04:46.680 --> 04:50.280
Because this is not a Select query this is a DDL command.

04:50.480 --> 04:54.720
So the SQL is going to tell you okay either I created it successfully or not.

04:54.760 --> 04:58.040
So now the question is where do I find now my view.

04:58.080 --> 05:03.840
Well if you go to the Object Explorer you can see over here underneath our database sales DB.

05:03.880 --> 05:09.760
We have here something called tables where we are used to query those tables, but beneath it we have

05:09.800 --> 05:11.000
as well our views.

05:11.000 --> 05:16.440
So if you check the views and expand it now, we are not seeing a view because we just created the view

05:16.480 --> 05:17.080
here.

05:17.080 --> 05:18.960
So go over here and refresh.

05:18.960 --> 05:22.840
And once you do that you will see the newly created view.

05:22.840 --> 05:25.080
So this is the one that we just created.

05:25.080 --> 05:30.040
So now what we can do we can go and create a new query and let's go and just query the view.

05:30.040 --> 05:36.920
So select star from so V monthly summary.

05:37.600 --> 05:38.840
Let's go and execute it.

05:38.840 --> 05:42.000
And now as you can see we are getting now the result of the view.

05:42.000 --> 05:46.160
And I'm accessing now this logic from completely external query.

05:46.160 --> 05:50.680
So now I can think about the view as any other table that we have in the database.

05:50.680 --> 05:53.760
And again the big differences between the views and the tables.

05:53.920 --> 05:58.560
The tables has data has actual data and everything there is persisted.

05:58.560 --> 06:01.280
But the view is just an abstraction for me.

06:01.280 --> 06:07.360
And behind it there is like a query that goes to the table and query the tables in order to present

06:07.360 --> 06:08.110
the their results.

06:08.110 --> 06:10.350
But for me, I don't care about all those details.

06:10.350 --> 06:13.830
I can go immediately to the query over here and start querying.

06:13.830 --> 06:19.430
So now in order to create the total running sales, I don't have to create the CTE and sub queries.

06:19.430 --> 06:22.670
I just go and get, for example, our main query.

06:23.510 --> 06:24.910
Let's go back over here.

06:25.310 --> 06:29.830
So now instead of using the CTE I can go directly and access the view.

06:30.470 --> 06:33.870
So as you can see now my query is very simple.

06:33.870 --> 06:38.630
I'm doing immediately the step two without having to prepare the data first.

06:38.630 --> 06:41.750
So if I go and execute it I will get exact results.

06:41.790 --> 06:47.630
And now if you compare the query on top of the view like this with the CTE query, you can see that

06:47.630 --> 06:49.430
the city has more steps.

06:49.430 --> 06:53.870
And it is like a little bit more complicated than the query on top of the view.

06:53.870 --> 06:56.070
And this is exactly the benefit of the view.

06:56.110 --> 07:01.190
We reduce the complexity and it is very easy to consume from the point of view of users.

07:01.190 --> 07:05.950
So this is how you can put your logic in central place using views.

07:05.950 --> 07:08.510
And with that you have learned how we create a view.

07:08.550 --> 07:10.270
Now one more thing about the schemas.

07:10.270 --> 07:14.030
If you check our tables over here, they have all one schema.

07:14.030 --> 07:18.550
So we have sales customers sales employees, orders and so on.

07:18.590 --> 07:21.430
Our new view has the schema of dbo.

07:21.470 --> 07:27.230
If you create any object, whether it's table or view, and you don't specify a schema in a default

07:27.230 --> 07:28.430
schema called DBO.

07:28.470 --> 07:31.110
And now let's go back to our DDL scripts.

07:31.270 --> 07:34.030
So as you can see over here we didn't specify any schema.

07:34.030 --> 07:36.590
We just said okay this is the view name.

07:36.590 --> 07:41.350
And now in order to put our view in the correct schema we don't want it to be in the defaults.

07:41.350 --> 07:44.550
You have to go and specify the schema name in the DDL.

07:44.590 --> 07:50.990
And now in order to do that we go to the name of the view and we write the schema name and then separated

07:50.990 --> 07:51.750
with a dot.

07:51.790 --> 07:56.350
So the first one is the schema name and the second one is the view name.

07:56.350 --> 07:58.110
So now let's go and execute it.

07:58.150 --> 08:00.390
Now if you check over here you don't see anything new.

08:00.390 --> 08:04.590
But if you refresh you will find another view in the correct schema.

08:04.590 --> 08:07.870
So we have sales dot v monthly summary.

08:07.870 --> 08:09.630
And this is exactly what we want.

08:09.670 --> 08:14.270
So this is how you can assign a view or even a table to the correct schema.

08:14.270 --> 08:16.550
If you don't want to use the default one, the dbo.

08:16.590 --> 08:16.910
All right.

08:16.910 --> 08:19.710
So now the next step that you say, you know what I would like to clean up.

08:19.710 --> 08:22.390
I don't need those two views in my database.

08:22.390 --> 08:23.750
So how to delete a view.

08:23.790 --> 08:25.910
We can go and use the command drop.

08:25.910 --> 08:26.830
It is very simple.

08:26.830 --> 08:31.910
If you go and create a new query and you say drop and then you say what you want to drop, you want

08:31.910 --> 08:32.710
to drop a view.

08:32.710 --> 08:36.590
And then you have to specify the name and schema of the view.

08:36.590 --> 08:40.630
But now since it is that default schema debut, I don't have to write it down.

08:40.630 --> 08:43.230
So we can start immediately with the view name.

08:43.230 --> 08:46.390
So V monthly summary.

08:46.550 --> 08:47.190
So that's it.

08:47.190 --> 08:48.030
It's very simple.

08:48.430 --> 08:50.550
So now we go and execute it.

08:50.590 --> 08:51.870
It says it's completed.

08:51.870 --> 08:53.870
But as you can see nothing is changed.

08:53.870 --> 08:54.830
We go and refresh.

08:54.830 --> 08:59.550
And now we can see that the database did remove the view with the schema debut.

08:59.590 --> 09:00.670
So it's very simple.

09:00.670 --> 09:03.790
This is how you can drop a view in SQL okay.

09:03.830 --> 09:04.950
So now to the next step.

09:04.950 --> 09:09.150
Let's go back to our DDL of creating the view Sales monthly summary.

09:09.150 --> 09:15.070
And now you say you know what I would like to change the logic inside the view so how we can update

09:15.070 --> 09:15.790
this content.

09:15.830 --> 09:21.430
How I can update my query if you say let's go and for example delete this column, I need only three

09:21.430 --> 09:22.070
columns.

09:22.070 --> 09:23.830
So and you go execute it.

09:23.830 --> 09:28.510
The database say I cannot do it for you because we have already such a view.

09:28.510 --> 09:30.710
So SQL will not go and replace stuff.

09:30.750 --> 09:35.990
SQL going to say no, we have the same name and I cannot do anything for it.

09:35.990 --> 09:37.310
So how we can update the view?

09:37.350 --> 09:40.430
Well, in other databases like Postgres for example.

09:40.430 --> 09:41.270
It's very simple.

09:41.270 --> 09:45.950
You can go over here and say create or replace view.

09:45.950 --> 09:51.310
So it's like you are telling the database, create this view or if it already exists, then replace

09:51.310 --> 09:57.150
it and you will not get error in the Postgres, but in the SQL server it is a little bit more complicated.

09:57.150 --> 09:58.550
We don't have this command.

09:58.550 --> 09:59.750
So here you have two ways.

09:59.750 --> 10:02.390
Either you go over here and say, you know what.

10:02.870 --> 10:04.910
Let's first drop the view.

10:05.630 --> 10:07.790
So you go with the same name over here.

10:09.070 --> 10:12.070
And then what you're going to do, you're going to go and mark the drop view.

10:12.070 --> 10:14.910
So if you execute it like this, the view is going to be dropped.

10:14.910 --> 10:17.590
And then we recreate the view like this.

10:17.590 --> 10:19.430
So what we have done we destroyed the view.

10:19.430 --> 10:22.150
And then we recreate it using the new logic.

10:22.150 --> 10:24.790
Or you say you know what, I would like to have everything in one go.

10:24.830 --> 10:26.390
Like I don't want to do it in two steps.

10:26.390 --> 10:28.430
I would like to have everything in one command.

10:28.430 --> 10:33.590
And for that you have to use an SQL server, the T-sql that transact-sql.

10:33.590 --> 10:37.030
It is like an extension for SQL only in SQL server.

10:37.030 --> 10:41.550
Well, it's like programming where you can go and add variables or you can go and add checks.

10:41.550 --> 10:45.870
We will not do a deep dive in this language, but I would like to show you how to do it for the views.

10:45.870 --> 10:47.390
So just follow me with that.

10:47.430 --> 10:50.350
I'm going to go and replace the whole thing.

10:50.390 --> 10:57.150
And then we're going to say if and now we are checking the system catalog, if the object ID, and now

10:57.150 --> 10:59.390
we go and specify the view name.

10:59.390 --> 11:02.510
So let's go and copy the whole thing with the schema as well.

11:02.550 --> 11:06.030
And then we're going to say for SQL this is a view.

11:06.470 --> 11:10.390
So if this object exists so we are saying is not null.

11:10.590 --> 11:12.950
So that means it exists in the catalog.

11:12.950 --> 11:16.230
Then what is SQL should do should drop this view.

11:16.230 --> 11:17.870
So I'm going to say drop view.

11:17.870 --> 11:21.950
And it's like we have done it first and then semicolon.

11:22.110 --> 11:23.830
And then we say for SQL go.

11:24.030 --> 11:27.550
And with that we are saying for SQL that T-sql is done.

11:27.550 --> 11:29.510
So the logic is done.

11:29.510 --> 11:32.390
And after that we have the DDL for our view.

11:32.390 --> 11:38.150
So again what you are doing we are checking before creating the view whether the view exists.

11:38.150 --> 11:41.270
If it exists then we are telling SQL go and drop it.

11:41.270 --> 11:45.310
And if it doesn't exist, that means we haven't created this view yet.

11:45.550 --> 11:47.630
It is completely brand new view.

11:47.670 --> 11:50.910
Then this step can be skipped so there is nothing to drop.

11:51.030 --> 11:53.950
So now if you go and execute the whole thing, it will work.

11:53.950 --> 11:57.110
And of course if you go and refresh over here you still see the view.

11:57.510 --> 12:01.630
So SQL did destroy the table first and then recreated.

12:01.630 --> 12:03.270
So if you execute it again.

12:03.270 --> 12:07.710
So this is how you replace your logic in a view in SQL server.

12:07.710 --> 12:12.990
And with that you have learned all possible scenarios how to create a view, how to drop a view, and

12:12.990 --> 12:15.790
how to update the logic of a view.
